  "worked_solution": "First, we compute ${D}^{2}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}^{2} & =D\\cdot D \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2} & \\frac{1}{4} \\\\ 1 & \\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2} & \\frac{1}{4} \\\\ 1 & \\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2} & \\frac{1}{4} \\\\ 1 & \\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =D\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that ${D}^{2}=D,$ so $D$ is an idempotent matrix.\n\nWe can use this fact to compute ${D}^{3}$ quickly, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}^{3} & ={D}^{2}\\cdot D \\\\  & =D\\cdot D \\\\  & ={D}^{2} \\\\  & =D\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{D}^{3}=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2} & \\frac{1}{4} \\\\ 1 & \\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}].\n$$",
